+## Rotation of vectors and square matrices
+
+`change_basis` is available for `tvector<N>` and `tmatrix<N, N>`,
+consistently with the rotation of tensors (see [this page](tensors.html)).
+
+## Functions usable in device code
+
+Many functions are usable in device code:
+
+- the finite strain tools (`LogarithmicStrainHandler`, conversions of the
+  tangent operators, `pull_back`, `invert` of `st2tost2`, which returns
+  `NaN` values in device code if the tensor is singular);
+- the tensor views of symmetric tensors and the accessors of tensors;
+- the cubic spline and linear interpolations, the Aitken acceleration;
+- the non linear solvers (Broyden, Levenberg-Marquardt, Powell dog leg).
+
+## Aborting the resolution of a non linear system
+
+`TinyNonLinearSolverBase::abortResolution` makes the resolution fail.
+This allows to report errors in device code, where exceptions are not
+supported (see [this page](tfel-math.html)).
+
+## Fixed size arrays left uninitialized {#sec:tfel:5.2:tfel_math:uninitialized}
+
+By default, the values of the fixed size mathematical objects (`fsarray`,
+`tvector`, `tmatrix`, `stensor`, `tensor`, `st2tost2`, `t2tot2`,
+`t2tost2`, `st2tot2`, etc.) are set to zero by their default
+constructors. This is unchanged.
+
+Those objects now provide an explicit constructor which leaves their
+values uninitialized. This constructor is selected by the
+`tfel::math::uninitialized` tag:
+
+~~~~{.cxx}
+tmatrix<6, 6, double> m(tfel::math::uninitialized);
+~~~~
+
+This constructor is meant for objects whose values are all assigned
+before being read, when the compiler can't remove the initialization by
+itself. This is typically the case of the data members of an object
+which are assigned in a method called after the constructor, and of the
+objects built on the stack of a kernel running on a GPU, where writing
+those zeros is not negligible.
+
+The values of class type, such as quantities or nested arrays, are built
+by their default constructors.
+
+### Workspaces of the non linear solvers
+
+The workspaces allocated on the stack by the non linear solvers (see
+the `StackAllocatedTinyNonLinearSolverWorkspace` class and the classes
+derived from it) leave uninitialized:
+
+- the residual (`fzeros`), which the `computeResidual` method computes
+  before it is used,
+- the jacobian matrix (`jacobian`) of the Newton-Raphson algorithm
+  (`TinyNewtonRaphsonSolver`), which is computed at each iteration.
+
+The estimate of the unknowns (`zeros`) and the correction
+(`delta_zeros`), which may be read before being computed, are still
+initially null.
+
+## Products with the derivatives of the product of two tensors and of the Cauchy-Green tensors
+
+Most components of the fourth order tensors returned by
+`t2tot2<N, T>::tpld(B)`, `t2tot2<N, T>::tprd(A)`,
+`t2tost2<N, T>::dCdF(F)` and `t2tost2<N, T>::dBdF(F)` are null whatever
+the values of their argument. In 3D, only \(27\) of the \(81\)
+components of the derivatives of the product of two tensors and \(27\)
+of the \(54\) components of the derivatives of the Cauchy-Green tensors
+are not null.
+
+The products of those objects with a fourth order tensor (`t2tot2`,
+`t2tost2`, `st2tot2`, `st2tost2`), or with one another, no longer compute
+the terms which are null by construction:
+
+~~~~{.cxx}
+// each component is the sum of 3 terms in 3D, 9 terms were computed before
+const t2tost2<3u, real> dS_dF = dS_dFe * t2tot2<3u, real>::tpld(iFp);
+~~~~
+
+The opposite of those derivatives and their products or divisions by a
+scalar have the same null components: the terms which are null by
+construction are not computed either in
+`-t2tost2<3u, real>::dCdF(Fe) * dFe_dF / 2`.
+
+The other terms are summed in the same order as in the standard product.
+The results are thus unchanged, with the following exceptions:
+
+- a non finite value of an operand is only propagated by the terms which
+  are computed. The result is still non finite, but fewer of its
+  components may be affected.
+- a null component of the result may have a different sign.
+- a compiler contracting floating-point expressions (see the
+  `-ffp-contract` option of `gcc` and `clang`) may fuse different
+  multiplications and additions, which may change the last bits of the
+  result.
+
+The standard product is still used when:
+
+- the derivative is stored in a fourth order tensor before the product
+  (`const t2tot2<3u, real> dFe_dF = t2tot2<3u, real>::tpld(iFp);`). The
+  derivative can be stored as an expression to avoid this
+  (`const auto dFe_dF = t2tot2<3u, real>::tpld(iFp);`).
+- the derivative is composed with a fourth order tensor by the overloads
+  of `tpld` and `tprd` which take two arguments.
+
+The null components of an expression are described by the
+`SparsityPattern` metafunction, declared in the
+`TFEL/Math/ExpressionTemplates/SparseProductExpr.hxx` header.
+
