 The `TFEL/Config` library is mostly used internally by `TFEL` and
 provides some information related to the project.
 
+# Device code
+
+The `TFEL/Config/TFELConfig.hxx` header defines the following macros:
+
+- `TFEL_HOST_DEVICE` declares a function callable on the host and in
+  device code (`__host__ __device__` with `CUDA` and `HIP`, nothing
+  otherwise).
+- `TFEL_DEVICE_CODE` is defined when device code is compiled: device
+  compilation pass of `CUDA` and `HIP` sources, device compilation of
+  `SYCL` sources. Some compilers translate host and device code in a
+  single pass: `nvc++ -stdpar=gpu`, `nvc++ -mp=gpu`, `nvc++ -acc=gpu` and
+  the generic compiler of AdaptiveCpp. With those compilers,
+  `TFEL_DEVICE_CODE` is defined in the whole translation unit.
+- `TFEL_NO_REPORT_CONTRACT_VIOLATION` is defined when contract violations
+  can't be reported, in particular in device code and in the whole `SYCL`
+  sources.
+- `TFEL_STATIC_VARIABLE(v)` gives the value of a constexpr static variable
+  `v` in code which may be compiled as device code: the variable itself on
+  the host, a temporary copy `(decltype(v){v})` when `TFEL_DEVICE_CODE` is
+  defined, since device code can't refer to a constexpr static variable
+  defined in host code. `MFront` uses it for the static variables read by
+  the code blocks of the behaviours: a reference derived from such a
+  variable, for instance the result of `std::min(Tref, T)`, is only valid
+  until the end of the full expression, and its address can't be taken.
+
+`TFEL_DEVICE_CODE` allows to select the code which can't be executed on
+the device, for instance when throwing an exception, while keeping the
+same behaviour on the host.
+
